//
// A TUPLE MEMBER RELEASES ONCE, WHATEVER IT IS DECLARED AND BESIDE (`@FR-N-Shape`).  A tuple member
// declared nullable, one beside any neighbour, and each member of a nested tuple copy is released
// once.  Each section keeps its own cases and receipt.
//
// ── a-tuple-member-declared-nullable-releases-once ──
//
// `heap.md (H-Drop)` runs the hook once per resource and moves the responsibility with a copy
// (`binding.md (B-Copy)`), so `t = (s, 5); u = t` releases ONCE.  Writing the member's type as
// NULLABLE must not change that — `@FR-N-Shape` says a `τ?` is a nullability bit over the same
// runtime shape, and a release is a shape question.
//
// The hand-off is recognised by resolving the copy's tuple-MEMBER source to the work-ref
// backing it, and the tuple's TYPE is where that pairing lives.  A declared type says the
// SHAPE and cannot say what the value in hand owns, because it was written before that value
// existed — so the site that adopts an annotation over a literal's own type has to carry the
// literal's deps across.  It did not, and only for a member the annotation makes nullable: a
// declared `(S, integer)` is `is_equal` to the literal's type (`is_equal` collapses deps) and
// is never converted at all, so it keeps its backing by not taking that path.  The `?` is what
// makes the two types differ, which is what routes the binding through the conversion, which is
// what dropped the backing — after which the copy had no work-ref to hand the release to and
// the resource was released TWICE, both backends, silently.
//
// c_dense and c_inferred are the cells that pin the axis on the `?` rather than on the
// annotation: both are declared-or-not tuples of the same shape and both were always correct.
// Without them a reader would take "a declared tuple loses its backing" from this file, which
// is the scope `formal/heap-history.md` D-heap-1 carried before it was measured.
//
// @falsified-at: 7e61fc15c — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0,
//   native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0.
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-tuple-member-declared-nullable-releases-once.patch —
//   interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0,
//   native 1 assertion failures -> 0.
// CHANNEL: the assertion count, and the exit code with it, IDENTICALLY on both backends —
//   this is not a backend divergence, both released twice.  NOT the leak channel: the second
//   release is of a record that genuinely exists, so nothing leaks and a run scored on leaks
//   learns nothing here.  What is lost is that the resource behind those two records is ONE,
//   which only an assertion on the hook's own trace can see.
// ARMED: no instrument.  The guard writes each hook call to its own trace file and asserts
//   the string, so an ordinary run on either backend reports it.
// WITNESS: on the control `c_null` drains `alivefalse,1,1` where the fix drains
//   `alivefalse,1` — the resource behind one droppable released twice.  It is the first cell,
//   so the run stops there; the others were measured by scoring the file with the assertions
//   replaced by prints, rather than assumed: `c_at_one` reads `alivefalse,4,4` against
//   `alivefalse,4`, and `c_two_null` reads `alivefalsefalse,51,52,51,52` against
//   `alivefalsefalse,51,52`.  That last one is worth reading closely — BOTH members release
//   twice, not one twice and the other once, which says the backing was LOST for the whole
//   tuple rather than paired to the wrong work-ref.  A dep list that named the wrong member
//   would have produced the asymmetric trace instead.
// HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal and expectations are unmoved — equal on both trees and on
//   both backends.  The cells the fix must NOT touch stay put: `c_dense` and `c_inferred`
//   pass on the control (they never take the conversion path), `c_absent` releases nothing on
//   either, and the `c_two` CONTROL reads two releases on both.
//
// The trace file is this guard's own — and its own means THIS file, not this family: seven
// corpus guards write one, and two concurrent runs of the SAME guard clobber each other's
// (see `scripts/falsify.sh`, which now says so on its unclean path).
//
// The ORDER within one tuple is reverse member order, as a struct's fields are (loft#1588): the
// cells with two droppables pinned the forward order until then, which is what the backings'
// registration at the function's head produced, not a rule.
//
// ── a-tuple-member-releases-once-beside-any-neighbour ──
//
// `heap.md (H-Drop)` runs the hook once per resource and moves the responsibility with a copy
// (`binding.md (B-Copy)`), so `t = (s, 5); u = t` releases ONCE — and what sits BESIDE the
// droppable in the tuple is not a fact about the droppable.  A `text` neighbour, a value-enum
// neighbour, a text neighbour between two droppables: each is the same release question with a
// different member alongside it, and each released TWICE, both backends, silently.
//
// The hand-off is recognised by resolving the copy's tuple-MEMBER source to the work-ref
// backing it, and the pairing that read needs — THIS work-ref backs THIS member — is the one
// thing a tuple's type stops carrying.  A tuple has no dep list of its own, so giving the
// VARIABLE its deps writes the whole union into every member (`Type::with_deps`), and the read
// was left counting: the k-th dep backs the k-th heap leaf.  Counting is wrong in both
// directions and neither predicate is a superset of the other.  A `text` or a value-enum member
// contributes a dep without being a heap leaf, so the list is LONGER than the leaves; a text
// LITERAL member has a dep slot that stays empty, so widening the walk to "has a dep slot"
// makes the list SHORTER than the walk.  The count check then declined, the release was never
// handed to the copy, and both records ran the hook.
//
// So the pairing is carried instead of inferred: `Vars::tuple_backings` records each heap
// leaf's own backing at `depend_all`, the one site that writes the union over them, and
// `scopes::tuple_member_backing` reads it.  Recorded as the JOIN over the variable's
// assignments, which c_reassigned is the cell for — the reader has only the variable, so a
// tuple variable assigned twice has no single answer and taking the latest one loses a release
// outright rather than duplicating one.
//
// The c_declared cells are the same question at a SECOND site, and they are here because they
// were found by this file's own over-wide cell rather than by the report.  Writing the tuple's
// type out routes the binding through the conversion that adopts an ANNOTATION over the
// literal's own type, and that site carried the literal's deps across as the union for the
// same reason this one wrote it: a tuple has no dep list of its own, so `with_deps_of` cannot
// reach one.  `Type::with_member_deps_of` is the tuple-aware form — it pairs the members up —
// and without it the annotated spellings still released twice while every inferred one had
// been closed.
//
// @falsified-at: 7d6192134 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0,
//   native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0.  c_text is the first moving cell on
//   both backends (the run stops at its first failed assertion); the other sixteen were
//   measured on that same build by scoring the file with the assertions replaced by prints.
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-tuple-member-releases-once-beside-any-neighbour.patch —
//   interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0,
//   native 1 assertion failures -> 0.
// CHANNEL: the assertion count, and the exit code with it, IDENTICALLY on both backends —
//   this is not a divergence, both released twice.  NOT the leak channel: the second release is
//   of a record that genuinely exists, so nothing leaks and a run scored on leaks learns
//   nothing here.  What is lost is that the resource behind those two records is ONE.
// ARMED: no instrument.  The guard writes each hook call to its own trace file and asserts the
//   string, so an ordinary run on either backend reports it.
// WITNESS: on the control c_text drains `alive11wx,11,11` where the fix drains `alive11wx,11`
//   — one resource, the hook twice.  It is the first cell, so the run stops there; the rest
//   were measured by scoring the file with the assertions replaced by prints rather than
//   assumed.  SEVENTEEN of the 24 cells move.  c_between reads `alive1112,11,12,11,12` against
//   `alive1112,11,12` — BOTH droppables twice, not one twice and the other once, which is what
//   says the pairing was not read at all rather than paired to the wrong work-ref; c_chain
//   reads `alive8181,81,81,81`, three records of one resource, one per hop; c_loop reads each
//   iteration's resource twice; c_nested_nullable reads six releases of two resources.
// HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal and expectations are unmoved — equal on both trees and on
//   both backends.  SEVEN cells hold, and each is there to hold: c_literal_text and c_scalar
//   (their dep list already matched the leaf walk), c_two_droppables, c_param, c_from_call,
//   c_plain, and c_reassigned — which read FOUR releases on both trees, because a variable
//   assigned two tuples has no single pairing and the join declines rather than answer with
//   the latest one.  c_reassigned reads two since loft#1563, whose hand-off does not go through
//   the join.
//
// The ORDER of the releases in each trace is `(H-Drop)`'s: owners in reverse declaration order,
// and one structure's members in reverse member order, as a struct's fields are.  Seven cells
// pinned another order until loft#1588 — whatever the backings' registration at the function's
// head produced — and read the rule's since that fix placed each backing at its tuple's turn.
//
// The trace file is this guard's own — its own means THIS file, not this family: several corpus
// guards write one, and two concurrent runs of the SAME guard clobber each other's.
//
// ── a-nested-tuple-copy-releases-each-member-once ──
//
// `heap.md (H-Drop)` runs the hook once per resource and moves the responsibility with a copy
// (`binding.md (B-Copy)`), so `t = ((s, 1), 2); u = t` releases ONCE.  `layout.md (L-Tuple)`
// makes a tuple a synthetic struct, so a tuple INSIDE a tuple is a container inside a
// container and the depth is not a semantic axis — a release is one per resource at every
// depth.
//
// The hand-off is recognised by resolving the copy's tuple-MEMBER source to the work-ref
// backing it, and the tuple's TYPE is where that pairing lives: the dep lists are unioned
// across the tuple's heap members and spread back into each of them, in MEMBER order.  Two
// things had to be read to find a nested leaf in that list and neither was:
//
//   * the count and the index were taken over ONE LEVEL's members while the list runs over
//     every heap leaf THROUGH the nesting, so a tuple with an inner tuple either declined
//     (a level count of 1 against a list of 2) or indexed the list at the wrong place;
//   * a nested copy reads each leaf through a HOLD (`_tuphold_N`) whose own dep list names
//     the base variable and nothing else, so the tuple whose type carries the pairing is one
//     or more holds further out and the source named none of them.
//
// Either way the copy had no work-ref to hand the release to and the resource was released
// TWICE, both backends, silently.
//
// c_outer_and_inner is the cell that says the index is positional over member order and not
// the order the work-refs were minted in: `(a, (b, 2))` parses the inner literal FIRST, so
// the inner member's work-ref has the LOWER number while the dep list names the outer
// member's first.  A read that sorted, or that trusted the numbering, passes every other
// cell here and fails this one.  c_both_in_one is its twin for the per-leaf half — with one
// droppable the k-th dep is right by accident, and with two in the same inner tuple, naming
// the wrong one releases one resource twice and the other never.
//
// @falsified-at: bff57af66 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0,
//   native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-nested-tuple-copy-releases-each-member-once.patch —
//   interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0,
//   native 1 assertion failures -> 0.  Re-derived against the COMMITTED fix, so the control
//   it names is the tree the guard was written for and not whatever the working copy held.
// CHANNEL: the assertion count, and the exit code with it, IDENTICALLY on both backends —
//   this is not a backend divergence, both released twice.  NOT the leak channel: the second
//   release is of a record that genuinely exists, so nothing leaks and a run scored on leaks
//   learns nothing here.  What is lost is that the resource behind those records is ONE, which
//   only an assertion on the hook's own trace can see.
// ARMED: no instrument.  The guard writes each hook call to its own trace file and asserts the
//   string, so an ordinary run on either backend reports it.
// WITNESS: on the control `c_inner` drains `a1,1,1` where the fix drains `a1,1`.  It is the
//   first cell, so the run stops there; the others were measured by scoring the file with the
//   assertions replaced by prints rather than assumed.  `c_two_inner` reads `a1112,11,12,11,12`
//   against `a1112,11,12`; `c_both_in_one` reads `a2122,21,22,21,22`; `c_depth3` reads
//   `a41,41,41`; `c_nullable_inner` reads `afalse,81,81`; `c_vector_member` reads
//   `a1312,131,131`; `c_chain` reads `a141,141,141,141` — three releases, one per name the
//   value wore.  `c_outer_and_inner` reads `a3132,32,31,31,32`: BOTH members released twice,
//   including the outer one, which is why `formal/heap-history.md` D-heap-1's earlier scope ("not
//   nesting as such but a droppable reached THROUGH an inner tuple") did not survive being
//   measured with a droppable on each side.
// HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal and expectations are unmoved — equal on both trees and on
//   both backends.  The cells the fix must NOT touch stay put: `c_outer_only`, `c_flat` and
//   `c_flat_two` pass on the control (the level count and the leaf count agree where there is
//   no nesting), `c_no_copy` releases once with nothing copied out of it, `c_struct_field`
//   holds the D-heap-2 neighbour at one, and the `c_two_indep` CONTROL reads two releases on
//   both.
//
// Two neighbouring shapes are deliberately ABSENT because they still release twice and
// `formal/heap-history.md` D-heap-1 carries them: a tuple with a dep-carrying `text` member beside the
// droppable (`t = ((s, 1), w)`), where the list counts the text's dep and the leaf walk does
// not, so the counts disagree and the pairing is unread; and a copy off a tuple PARAMETER,
// where the deps are the CALLER's and the argument rule is the one that applies.  Asserting
// their present answer would pin a defect into the suite.
//
// The trace file is this guard's own — and its own means THIS file, not this family: several
// corpus guards write one, and two concurrent runs of the SAME guard clobber each other's
// (see `scripts/falsify.sh`, which says so on its unclean path).
//
// The ORDER within one tuple is reverse member order, as a struct's fields are (loft#1588): the
// cells with two droppables pinned the forward order until then, which is what the backings'
// registration at the function's head produced, not a rule.

fn trace_path() -> text { return "loft_nullmember_trace.tmp"; }

fn note(s: text) {
  f = file(trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  if cur != "" { cur += ","; }
  cur += s;
  bytes: vector<u8> = [];
  for c in cur { bytes += [c as u8 ?? 0]; }
  if !write_bytes(trace_path(), bytes) { println("trace write failed"); }
}

fn drain() -> text {
  f = file(trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  empty: vector<u8> = [];
  if !write_bytes(trace_path(), empty) { println("trace clear failed"); }
  return cur;
}

struct NmH { id: integer }
fn OpDrop(self: NmH) { note("{self.id}"); }
fn nm_mk(id: integer) -> NmH { return NmH { id: id }; }
struct NmS { h: NmH }

// The defect: the member's type is written NULLABLE, so the declared and inferred types
// differ and the binding is converted — which is where the backing was lost.
fn c_null() { s = NmS { h: nm_mk(1) }; t: (NmS?, integer) = (s, 5); u = t; note("alive{u.0 == null}"); }
// CONTROL — the same tuple declared NON-nullable.  Always correct, and the reason is the
// point: `is_equal` collapses deps, so this type never differs from the literal's and never
// takes the conversion path at all.
fn c_dense() { s = NmS { h: nm_mk(2) }; t: (NmS, integer) = (s, 5); u = t; note("alive{u.0.h.id}"); }
// CONTROL — no annotation at all.  The literal's own type is used verbatim.
fn c_inferred() { s = NmS { h: nm_mk(3) }; t = (s, 5); u = t; note("alive{u.0.h.id}"); }
// A nullable member at index 1, with a scalar before it: the backing is read POSITIONALLY over
// the heap members, so a scalar ahead of it must contribute nothing.
fn c_at_one() { s = NmS { h: nm_mk(4) }; t: (integer, NmS?) = (7, s); u = t; note("alive{u.1 == null}"); }
// TWO nullable droppable members — the cell that tells a per-member pairing from `first()`.
// With one member the k-th dep is right by accident; with two, naming the wrong work-ref
// releases one resource twice and the other once.
fn c_two_null() { a = NmS { h: nm_mk(51) }; b = NmS { h: nm_mk(52) }; t: (NmS?, NmS?) = (a, b); u = t; note("alive{u.0 == null}{u.1 == null}"); }
// A nullable member that really IS absent releases nothing — there is no resource to release,
// and a fix that handed off a backing it had guessed would show up here.
fn c_absent() { t: (NmS?, integer) = (null, 5); u = t; note("absent{u.0 == null}"); }
// CONTROL — two independent resources release TWICE, in reverse declaration order.  Without it
// every cell above would pass on a harness that had simply stopped recording.
fn c_two() { x = NmS { h: nm_mk(91) }; y = NmS { h: nm_mk(92) }; note("alive{x.h.id}{y.h.id}"); }

fn test_a_tuple_member_declared_nullable_releases_once() {
  empty: vector<u8> = [];
  if !write_bytes(trace_path(), empty) { println("trace init failed"); }

  c_null(); got = drain();
  assert(got == "alivefalse,1", "a NULLABLE member releases once: {got}");

  c_dense(); got = drain();
  assert(got == "alive2,2", "CONTROL the same tuple declared non-nullable: {got}");

  c_inferred(); got = drain();
  assert(got == "alive3,3", "CONTROL no annotation at all: {got}");

  c_at_one(); got = drain();
  assert(got == "alivefalse,4", "a nullable member at index 1, a scalar before it: {got}");

  c_two_null(); got = drain();
  assert(got == "alivefalsefalse,52,51", "two nullable droppable members, each once: {got}");

  c_absent(); got = drain();
  assert(got == "absenttrue", "an absent nullable member releases nothing: {got}");

  c_two(); got = drain();
  assert(got == "alive9192,92,91", "CONTROL two resources release TWICE: {got}");

  if !delete(trace_path()) { println("trace cleanup failed"); }
  println("nullable tuple member ok");
}

fn s2_trace_path() -> text { return "loft_neighbour_trace.tmp"; }

fn s2_note(s: text) {
  f = file(s2_trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  if cur != "" { cur += ","; }
  cur += s;
  bytes: vector<u8> = [];
  for c in cur { bytes += [c as u8 ?? 0]; }
  if !write_bytes(s2_trace_path(), bytes) { println("trace write failed"); }
}

fn s2_drain() -> text {
  f = file(s2_trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  empty: vector<u8> = [];
  if !write_bytes(s2_trace_path(), empty) { println("trace clear failed"); }
  return cur;
}

struct NbH { id: integer }
fn OpDrop(self: NbH) { s2_note("{self.id}"); }
fn nb_mk(id: integer) -> NbH { return NbH { id: id }; }
enum NbVE { A, B }
enum NbSE { X { k: integer }, Y { k: integer } }

// The defect: a `text` neighbour.  The text contributes a dep of its own — the variable it
// borrows — so the dep list is one longer than the tuple's heap leaves.
fn c_text() { s = nb_mk(11); w = "wx"; t = (s, w); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.1}"); }
// The same neighbour BEFORE the droppable.  The dep list is in member order, so this moves the
// backing off the position `first()` would answer.
fn c_text_first() { s = nb_mk(21); w = "wx"; t = (w, s); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.1.id}{u.0}"); }
// A text BETWEEN two droppables — the cell that tells a per-member pairing from a count that
// happens to line up: naming the wrong work-ref releases one resource twice and the other once,
// and reading nothing releases both twice.
fn c_between() { a = nb_mk(11); w = "wx"; b = nb_mk(12); t = (a, w, b); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.2.id}"); }
// A value ENUM neighbour — the other member kind that carries a dep without being a heap leaf.
fn c_value_enum() { s = nb_mk(31); e = NbVE.B; t = (s, e); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}"); }
// CONTROL — a text LITERAL neighbour.  It has a dep SLOT and no dep, so the list is SHORTER
// than a "has a slot" walk would expect.  This is the cell a widened leaf predicate breaks, and
// it was always correct.
fn c_literal_text() { s = nb_mk(41); t = (s, "lit"); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.1}"); }
// CONTROL — a scalar neighbour contributes no dep at all.  Always correct.
fn c_scalar() { s = nb_mk(51); t = (s, 5); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}"); }
// CONTROL — two droppables and no dep-carrying neighbour.  Always correct, and the cell that
// says the count and the leaf walk agree when nothing else contributes.
fn c_two_droppables() { a = nb_mk(61); b = nb_mk(62); t = (a, b); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.1.id}"); }
// A NESTED tuple with a text beside it: the leaf ordinal runs through the nesting, so the text
// has to be skipped by the walk at the level it sits on and not at the leaf.
fn c_nested() { s = nb_mk(71); w = "wx"; t = ((s, 1), w); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.0.id}{u.1}"); }
// A CHAIN of copies.  Each hop re-reads the pairing, so a decline costs one release per hop —
// three records of one resource, which is what makes this cell worth its own line.
fn c_chain() { s = nb_mk(81); w = "wx"; t = (s, w); u = t; z = u; s2_note("alive{z.0.id}"); }
// A DESTRUCTURE after the copy — the third read site of the same pairing.
fn c_destructure() { s = nb_mk(91); w = "wx"; t = (s, w); u = t; (a, b) = u; s2_note("alive{a.id}{b}"); }
// The copy in a LOOP: one assignment site, run three times, so the pairing is recorded once and
// read on every iteration.
fn c_loop() { w = "wx"; for i in 0..3 { s = nb_mk(100 + i); t = (s, w); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}"); } }
// TWO tuples in one frame.  Their backings are different variables, so a table keyed by the
// tuple rather than by the member is what keeps them apart.
fn c_two_tuples() { a = nb_mk(111); b = nb_mk(112); w = "wx"; t1 = (a, w); t2 = (b, w); u1 = t1; u2 = t2; s2_note("alive{u1.0.id}{u2.0.id}"); }
// The tuple variable is assigned TWICE, with different backings.  The reader has only the
// variable, so there is no single pairing to record, and the join declines: taking the LATEST
// answer instead passes this cell's first half and LOSES the second resource's release entirely.
// Each member's release now moves through the scan's per-assignment `tuple_call_mint` instead
// (loft#1563), which does see which assignment is live, so each resource releases once.  It was
// a control that pinned both releasing twice.
fn c_reassigned() { a = nb_mk(131); w = "wx"; b = nb_mk(132); v = "wy"; t = (a, w); u = t; t = (b, v); z = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{z.0.id}"); }
// A STRUCT-ENUM member beside the text.  It IS a heap leaf, so the leaf walk counts it and the
// dep list counts it too — and the text between them still put the two out of step.
fn c_enum_beside() { s = nb_mk(141); w = "wx"; e = X { k: 3 }; t = (s, w, e); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.1}"); }
// A VECTOR member beside the text — a heap leaf of another kind, so the ordinal has to run past
// it the same way.
fn c_vector_beside() { s = nb_mk(151); w = "wx"; v: vector<integer> = [7, 8]; t = (s, w, v); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.2[1]}"); }
// SCALARS of the other widths beside the text: a float and a boolean contribute no dep, so they
// move the member positions without moving the dep list.
fn c_scalars_beside() { s = nb_mk(161); w = "wx"; f = 1.5; b = true; t = (s, w, f, b); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.3}"); }
// A NESTED tuple member whose own member is NULLABLE, beside the text — the two axes that each
// closed a shape of `heap-history.md` D-heap-1 on their own, crossed.
fn c_nested_nullable() { s = nb_mk(171); w = "wx"; h = nb_mk(172); inner: (NbH?, text) = (h, w); t = (s, w, inner); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.2.0.id ?? 0}"); }
// CONTROL — the tuple comes back from a CALL rather than from a literal in this frame.  Its
// members' backings are the callee's, and the return buffer's own release is what covers them,
// so this was always correct and must stay so.
fn c_from_call_mk() -> (NbH, text) { return (nb_mk(181), "wx"); }
fn c_from_call() { t = c_from_call_mk(); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.1}"); }
// The tuple's type written OUT, with the text still beside the droppable.  A declared type
// says the SHAPE and cannot say what the value in hand owns, so the site that adopts an
// annotation over a literal's own type has to carry the literal's deps across — and carrying
// them as the UNION is what left this cell releasing twice after the same site's nullable
// member was closed.  A union cannot be taken apart again.
fn c_declared() { h = nb_mk(201); w = "wx"; t: (NbH, text) = (h, w); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.1}"); }
// The same, with the annotation making the member NULLABLE.  `@FR-N-Shape`: a `τ?` is a
// nullability bit over the same runtime shape, and a release is a shape question.
fn c_declared_null() { h = nb_mk(211); w = "wx"; t: (NbH?, text) = (h, w); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id ?? 0}{u.1}"); }
// A declared tuple with TWO droppables and the text last — the cell that tells a per-member
// carry from one that hands every member the same list.
fn c_declared_two() { a = nb_mk(221); b = nb_mk(222); w = "wx"; t: (NbH?, NbH?, text) = (a, b, w); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id ?? 0}{u.1.id ?? 0}"); }
// A declared tuple nested inside an inferred one, both carrying a text.
fn c_declared_nested() { h = nb_mk(231); w = "wx"; inner: (NbH?, text) = (h, w); s = nb_mk(232); t = (s, w, inner); u = t; s2_note("alive{u.0.id}{u.2.0.id ?? 0}"); }
// CONTROL — two independent resources release TWICE with no tuple in sight.  Without it every
// cell above would pass on a harness that had simply stopped recording.
fn c_plain() { x = nb_mk(191); y = nb_mk(192); s2_note("alive{x.id}{y.id}"); }

fn test_a_tuple_member_releases_once_beside_any_neighbour() {
  empty: vector<u8> = [];
  if !write_bytes(s2_trace_path(), empty) { println("trace init failed"); }

  c_text(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive11wx,11", "a text neighbour releases once: {got}");

  c_text_first(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive21wx,21", "a text neighbour BEFORE the droppable: {got}");

  c_between(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive1112,12,11", "a text between two droppables, each once: {got}");

  c_value_enum(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive31,31", "a value-enum neighbour releases once: {got}");

  c_literal_text(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive41lit,41", "CONTROL a text LITERAL neighbour: {got}");

  c_scalar(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive51,51", "CONTROL a scalar neighbour: {got}");

  c_two_droppables(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive6162,62,61", "CONTROL two droppables, each once: {got}");

  c_nested(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive71wx,71", "a nested member with a text beside it: {got}");

  c_chain(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive81,81", "a chain of moves releases once: {got}");

  c_destructure(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive91wx,91", "a destructure after the copy: {got}");

  c_loop(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive100,100,alive101,101,alive102,102", "the copy in a loop: {got}");

  c_two_tuples(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive111112,112,111", "two tuples in one frame: {got}");


  c_reassigned(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive131132,132,131", "a reassigned tuple releases each resource once: {got}");

  c_enum_beside(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive141wx,141", "a struct-enum member beside the text: {got}");

  c_vector_beside(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive1518,151", "a vector member beside the text: {got}");

  c_scalars_beside(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive161true,161", "a float and a boolean beside the text: {got}");

  c_nested_nullable(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive171172,172,171", "a nested nullable member beside the text: {got}");

  c_from_call(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive181wx,181", "CONTROL the tuple comes back from a call: {got}");

  c_declared(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive201wx,201", "a DECLARED tuple with a text member: {got}");

  c_declared_null(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive211wx,211", "a declared NULLABLE member beside a text: {got}");

  c_declared_two(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive221222,222,221", "a declared tuple, two droppables and a text: {got}");

  c_declared_nested(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive232231,231,232", "a declared tuple nested in an inferred one: {got}");

  c_plain(); got = s2_drain();
  assert(got == "alive191192,192,191", "CONTROL two resources release twice: {got}");

  if !delete(s2_trace_path()) { println("trace cleanup failed"); }
  println("tuple member neighbour ok");
}

fn s3_trace_path() -> text { return "loft_nesttuple_trace.tmp"; }

fn s3_note(s: text) {
  f = file(s3_trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  if cur != "" { cur += ","; }
  cur += s;
  bytes: vector<u8> = [];
  for c in cur { bytes += [c as u8 ?? 0]; }
  if !write_bytes(s3_trace_path(), bytes) { println("trace write failed"); }
}

fn s3_drain() -> text {
  f = file(s3_trace_path());
  cur = f.content() ?? "";
  empty: vector<u8> = [];
  if !write_bytes(s3_trace_path(), empty) { println("trace clear failed"); }
  return cur;
}

struct NtH { id: integer }
fn OpDrop(self: NtH) { s3_note("{self.id}"); }
struct NtS { h: NtH }
fn nt_mk(id: integer) -> NtS { return NtS { h: NtH { id: id } }; }
struct NtHolder { t: (NtS, integer) }

// The defect: the droppable sits in the INNER tuple, so the copy reads it through a hold and
// the outer tuple's own level has one heap member against a dep list of one leaf.
fn c_inner() { s = nt_mk(1); t = ((s, 1), 2); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.0.h.id}"); }
// One droppable per inner tuple — two holds, two leaves, and the second leaf is the cell a
// `first()`-shaped read gets wrong.
fn c_two_inner() { a = nt_mk(11); b = nt_mk(12); t = ((a, 1), (b, 2)); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.0.h.id}{u.1.0.h.id}"); }
// TWO droppables in ONE inner tuple — the per-leaf pairing read through a single hold.
fn c_both_in_one() { a = nt_mk(21); b = nt_mk(22); t = ((a, b), 2); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.0.h.id}{u.0.1.h.id}"); }
// A droppable in the OUTER position beside one in the inner tuple.  The outer member's copy
// reads no hold at all, and the dep list still names it FIRST although the inner literal's
// work-ref was minted first — member order, not mint order.
fn c_outer_and_inner() { a = nt_mk(31); b = nt_mk(32); t = (a, (b, 2)); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.h.id}{u.1.0.h.id}"); }
// Depth three: a chain of two holds, which is what says the walk is a chain and not one step.
fn c_depth3() { a = nt_mk(41); t = (((a, 1), 2), 3); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.0.0.h.id}"); }
// The inner member written NULLABLE.  `@FR-N-Shape` makes a `τ?` a nullability bit over the
// same runtime shape, so the nesting answer must not depend on it.
fn c_nullable_inner() { s = nt_mk(81); t: ((NtS?, integer), integer) = ((s, 1), 2); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.0 == null}"); }
// A VECTOR leaf beside the nested droppable: a vector is a heap leaf and carries a dep of its
// own, so it must be COUNTED, and the record's index sits after it.
fn c_vector_member() { s = nt_mk(131); v: vector<integer> = [1, 2]; t = ((s, 1), v); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.0.h.id}{len(u.1)}"); }
// A chain of copies through the nested shape — one resource however many names it wears.
fn s3_c_chain() { s = nt_mk(141); t = ((s, 1), 2); u = t; w = u; s3_note("a{w.0.0.h.id}"); }
// CONTROL — a nested tuple whose droppable sits in the OUTER position with nothing droppable
// beside it.  Always correct: one heap leaf, a list of one.
fn c_outer_only() { a = nt_mk(51); t = (a, (1, 2)); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.h.id}"); }
// CONTROL — the FLAT case.  The leaf walk must agree with the level read where there is no
// nesting, or this fix moves an answer that was already right.
fn c_flat() { s = nt_mk(61); t = (s, 5); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.h.id}"); }
// CONTROL — flat, a scalar between two droppables: a scalar contributes no leaf and no dep.
fn c_flat_two() { a = nt_mk(71); b = nt_mk(72); t = (a, 7, b); u = t; s3_note("a{u.0.h.id}{u.2.h.id}"); }
// CONTROL — the nested literal ALONE, nothing copied out of it.  A fix that handed off a
// backing it had guessed suppresses a release that nothing else performs, and shows up here.
fn c_no_copy() { s = nt_mk(91); t = ((s, 1), 2); s3_note("a{t.0.0.h.id}"); }
// CONTROL — D-heap-2's neighbour: a struct FIELD holding a tuple releases through the cascade.
fn c_struct_field() { s = nt_mk(151); h = NtHolder { t: (s, 5) }; s3_note("a{h.t.0.h.id}"); }
// CONTROL — two independent resources release TWICE, in reverse declaration order.  Without it
// every cell above would pass on a harness that had simply stopped recording.
fn c_two_indep() { x = nt_mk(101); y = nt_mk(102); s3_note("a{x.h.id}{y.h.id}"); }

fn test_a_nested_tuple_copy_releases_each_member_once() {
  empty: vector<u8> = [];
  if !write_bytes(s3_trace_path(), empty) { println("trace init failed"); }

  c_inner(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a1,1", "a droppable in the INNER tuple releases once: {got}");

  c_two_inner(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a1112,12,11", "one droppable per inner tuple, each once: {got}");

  c_both_in_one(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a2122,22,21", "two droppables in one inner tuple, each once: {got}");

  c_outer_and_inner(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a3132,32,31", "an outer droppable beside an inner one, each once: {got}");

  c_depth3(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a41,41", "a droppable three levels down releases once: {got}");

  c_nullable_inner(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "afalse,81", "a NULLABLE inner member releases once: {got}");

  c_vector_member(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a1312,131", "a vector leaf beside the nested droppable: {got}");

  s3_c_chain(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a141,141", "a chain of copies releases once: {got}");

  c_outer_only(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a51,51", "CONTROL an outer droppable, nothing droppable beside it: {got}");

  c_flat(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a61,61", "CONTROL the flat tuple: {got}");

  c_flat_two(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a7172,72,71", "CONTROL flat, a scalar between two droppables: {got}");

  c_no_copy(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a91,91", "CONTROL the nested literal alone releases once: {got}");

  c_struct_field(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a151,151", "CONTROL a struct field holding a tuple: {got}");

  c_two_indep(); got = s3_drain();
  assert(got == "a101102,102,101", "CONTROL two resources release TWICE: {got}");

  if !delete(s3_trace_path()) { println("trace cleanup failed"); }
  println("nested tuple copy ok");
}
